        "message": {
          "content": "**Recommended Action:** **waive_late_fee**\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n| Factor | Assessment |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Policy Guidance** | The company\u2019s policy permits waiving late fees when the customer demonstrates a documented hardship. Hospitalization for a week is a clear, serious hardship that typically qualifies. |\n| **Customer History** | The account is active and the payment history is \u201cgenerally on time\u201d with only one late payment. This indicates the missed payment is an outlier rather than a pattern of delinquency. |\n| **Nature of Hardship** | A week\u2011long hospital stay is an unforeseen medical emergency that most customers cannot control. It also directly impacted the ability to make the payment on time. |\n| **Financial Impact on Customer** | The customer notes they are \u201cstill catching up on medical bills,\u201d suggesting limited cash flow. Waiving a $60 fee provides immediate relief without materially affecting the company\u2019s bottom line. |\n| **Risk Management** | Granting a one\u2011time waiver for a genuine hardship does not set a risky precedent because the customer\u2019s overall behavior is positive. The action can be documented in the account notes, and the waiver can be tied to the specific incident (hospitalization) to prevent future misuse. |\n| **Customer Experience** | Compassionate handling of the situation will likely increase loyalty, improve the customer\u2019s perception of the brand, and reduce the chance of further escalations or negative feedback. |\n\n**Operational Steps**\n\n1. **Apply the Waiver** \u2013 Use the \u201cwaive_late_fee\u201d action to remove the $60 charge from Jordan Kim\u2019s account. |\n2. **Document the Reason** \u2013 Add a note to the account: \u201cLate fee waived on 2026\u201111\u201108 due to documented hardship (hospitalization for one week). Customer has otherwise a strong payment record.\u201d |\n3. **Confirm with Customer** \u2013 Send a courteous confirmation email or message informing Jordan that the fee has been removed, expressing empathy for their situation, and reminding them of upcoming payment due dates. |\n4. **Monitor** \u2013 Flag the account for a brief follow\u2011up (e.g., 30\u202fdays) to ensure the customer returns to regular payments and to close the loop on the hardship case.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nGiven the clear hardship, the customer\u2019s solid payment history, and the modest size of the fee, waiving the late fee aligns with policy, mitigates risk, and demonstrates good customer service. Therefore, the appropriate next step is to **waive_late_fee** and document the decision accordingly.",
